Day 17 – Resource Requests & Limits ⚙️
1. Requests

A request tells Kubernetes how much CPU and memory a container needs for scheduling.

Example:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 128Mi

Think:

Request = What the container needs.

Kubernetes uses the request when deciding which Node has enough available resources to run the Pod.

Pod
 ↓
Requests resources
 ↓
Scheduler finds suitable Node
 ↓
Pod gets scheduled
2. Limits

A limit defines the maximum CPU and memory a container is allowed to use.

resources:
  limits:
    cpu: 500m
    memory: 256Mi

Think:

Limit = Maximum the container can use.

So:

Request → Needed for scheduling
Limit   → Maximum allowed
3. CPU

CPU is measured in millicores (m).

1000m = 1 CPU
500m  = 0.5 CPU
100m  = 0.1 CPU

Example:

requests:
  cpu: 100m

limits:
  cpu: 500m

Means:

CPU needed for scheduling → 100m
Maximum CPU allowed       → 500m
CPU limit

If the container tries to use more CPU than its limit, CPU can be throttled.

Throttling = restricting/slowing CPU usage to the allowed limit.

4. Memory

Memory is commonly specified using Mi or Gi.

Example:

requests:
  memory: 128Mi

limits:
  memory: 256Mi

Means:

Memory needed for scheduling → 128Mi
Maximum memory allowed       → 256Mi

Unlike CPU, memory cannot simply be throttled.

If the container exceeds its memory limit, it can be:

OOMKilled

OOM = Out Of Memory

5. Complete YAML

We created resources-pod.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: resources-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: 100m
        memory: 128Mi
      limits:
        cpu: 500m
        memory: 256Mi

Apply:

kubectl apply -f resources-pod.yaml

Check:

kubectl get pod resources-pod

Example:

NAME            READY   STATUS
resources-pod   1/1     Running
6. Verify Resources

Command:

kubectl describe pod resources-pod

Important section:

Limits:
  cpu:     500m
  memory:  256Mi

Requests:
  cpu:     100m
  memory:  128Mi

Our Pod was successfully scheduled to:

Node: node01

This demonstrates that Kubernetes successfully found a Node where the requested resources could be accommodated.

